LICENCE DIRECTION — APACHE-2.0 INTO GPL-3.0 IS ONE-WAY
======================================================

RAFFLES is distributed under GPL-3.0-only, the Outram Park workspace default.

Apache-2.0 is one-way compatible with GPLv3: Apache-2.0 code may be
incorporated into a GPLv3 work, and the combined result is governed by GPLv3.
The reverse does not hold — GPLv3 is not compatible with Apache-2.0, and
Apache-2.0 alone cannot satisfy GPLv3's terms. Concretely:

  * Code may flow  RAVEN (Apache-2.0)  ->  RAFFLES (GPL-3.0-only).
  * Code may NOT flow  RAFFLES (GPL-3.0-only)  ->  RAVEN (Apache-2.0).

Do not contribute RAFFLES code, patches, or translated files upstream to
RAVEN, and do not offer RAFFLES code to anyone under Apache-2.0 terms. Once a
translation lands here it is GPLv3 and it stays GPLv3. If the RAVEN project
ever wants something from this work, it has to be rewritten by them from the
specification, or separately relicensed by every RAFFLES copyright holder —
neither of which an AI assistant or a contributor may arrange unilaterally.

This restriction is on the direction of code flow, not on ideas: reading
RAVEN's papers and documentation and implementing the published algorithms is
unaffected.


PER-FILE ATTRIBUTION HEADERS (workspace rule)
=============================================

Every RAFFLES source file that is derived from a RAVEN source file must carry
an attribution header naming the upstream project, the upstream source file,
the version/commit it was taken from, the copyright holder, and the licence.
Do not strip these headers during refactors. A worked example header — copy it
verbatim and edit the fields — is in this crate's `CLAUDE.md`.

No RAVEN source has been ported into this crate yet. It is a documented
scaffold: module boundaries, licensing and provenance only.


